Unsigned wraparound has caused real production incidents — a classic pattern is subtracting sizes that don't behave as expected:

```c
unsigned int remaining = buffer_used - bytes_to_send;
// if bytes_to_send > buffer_used, this doesn't go negative --
// it wraps to a huge positive number (near UINT_MAX)
memcpy(dest, src, remaining); // reads/writes far past the buffer
```

This exact shape (unsigned subtraction going 'negative' and wrapping to a huge value, then used as a size) is behind real buffer-overflow CVEs. The fix is checking `bytes_to_send > buffer_used` *before* subtracting, or using a signed type with an explicit bounds check.
